## 1. Executive Summary

This report is a research plan for analysing the Information Technology (IT) sector of the US stock market. It explains why I chose the sector, what the first round of public research shows, which indicators I will track, which methods I will use and why, how the work is split across seven days, and what could go wrong.

I chose IT because it is where the numbers are most extreme right now. According to FactSet's 16 October 2026 Earnings Insight, IT companies in the S&P 500 are on course for third-quarter earnings growth of 65.1%, compared with 29.0% for the whole index. Revenue growth is 40.6% against 12.6%. Global chip sales passed $1 trillion for the year through August, the first time ever. At the same time, the 10-year Treasury yield is about 5.24%, its highest in more than two decades, and the Federal Reserve raised rates in September. A sector with that much growth and that much rate pressure is a good place to practise research design.

The plan uses a hybrid design. Macro indicators set the context, fundamental and valuation analysis tests whether prices are supported by earnings, technical analysis reads trend and momentum, and scenarios turn the evidence into a view. I specify eight indicators, three of them core, and I show worked examples of the calculations with real numbers so that every step can be checked.

