### 5.2 Interpretation guide

- **Valuation:** a forward P/E in the top decile of its ten-year range leaves little margin for error, but is not a timing signal by itself.
- **Earnings:** rising estimates with a high share of positive surprises supports a trend. Falling estimates while prices rise is a more fragile, valuation-driven rally.
- **Rates:** a sustained rise in the real yield is a headwind. A fall is supportive but may reflect weak growth, so it is checked against the PMI.
- **Confirmation rule:** a conclusion is robust only when at least three of the five scorecard categories (Appendix A) agree.

## 6. Research Methodologies and Rationale

No single method answers all four questions, so the plan combines complementary methods.

| Method | How it will be applied | Why it was selected | Known limitation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Top-down macro analysis | Start with growth, inflation, rates, dollar and policy and trace the effect to the sector (Table 6) | Answers RQ2. Rates matter greatly for the sector | Macro relationships change over time |
| Bottom-up fundamental analysis | Review revenue, margins, EPS growth and revisions for the sector and key companies; compare valuation with history | Answers RQ3. Earnings drive long-term returns | Depends on estimates, which get revised |
| Technical analysis | Moving averages, relative strength, momentum and drawdown | Answers RQ1. Shows what investors are actually doing | Backward-looking and partly subjective |
| Quantitative analysis | Rolling correlations and a regression of sector returns on market returns and yield changes (H1) | Gives objective, testable evidence | Correlation is not causation; sample-sensitive |
| Sentiment and positioning | VIX, investor surveys, share of Buy ratings, breadth | Detects crowded trades | Noisy over short periods |
| Scenario analysis | Bull, base and bear cases with triggers linked to indicators | Answers RQ4. Handles uncertainty | Judgement-based probabilities |

*Table 9. Methods and rationale.*

The analysis works as a funnel: macro sets the environment, fundamentals test whether prices are justified, technical and sentiment evidence shows current behaviour, quantitative tests check assumed relationships, and scenarios combine everything into a view with explicit triggers. Using several methods lowers the chance of confirmation bias, because a conclusion must survive evidence from different angles.

